TY  - JOUR
AU  - Dias, Rafaela
AU  - Lui, Lizandro
AU  - Lima, Luciana Leite
AU  - Barbosa de Aguiar, Rafael
TI  - Designing under uncertainty: cybersecurity policy architectures in Brazil and Chile
T2  - POLICY DESIGN AND PRACTICE
M3  - Article
M3  - Early Access
AB  - How is uncertainty reflected in the architecture of policy mixes? Although policy design scholarship widely acknowledges that instruments are selected under conditions of imperfect information, the observable imprints of uncertainty on policy design remain underexplored. This article proposes an operational framework that connects conceptual types of uncertainty to identifiable markers in policy mixes and applies it to a comparative analysis of cybersecurity policies from Brazil and Chile. The empirical analysis codes 16 national-level legal and strategic documents enacted between 1999 and 2025, using Hood's NATO typology to classify instruments by the governing resource through which they operate. Cybersecurity provides a productive domain in which the effects of unstable problem framing, fragmented authority, and rapid technological change are particularly visible. The analysis identifies two distinct trajectories: Brazil's portfolio reflects incremental layering and dispersed institutionalization, while Chile's recent reforms exhibit a more concentrated and packaged design. The findings suggest that uncertainty may narrow, rather than expand, the feasible design space, leading governments to rely on institutionally familiar authority instruments, an empirical pattern that the article develops as a broader contribution to policy design theory.
