TY  - JOUR
AU  - Oyarzun Serrano, Lorena
TI  - The Pacific in Chile's Foreign Policy: A Tool to Reinforce Open Regionalism
T2  - LATIN AMERICAN POLICY
M3  - Article
AB  - The reconfiguration of power hierarchies has transformed the Pacific Rim, mainly China, into one of the most-dynamic and fastest-growing regions. In line and consistent with its international-insertion strategy based on open regionalism, Chile has developed commercial links with Pacific countries, including the United States, China, Japan, South Korea, Australia, Canada, and Vietnam. It also participates fully in the Pacific Alliance-a regional cooperation mechanism that includes Colombia, Mexico, and Peru, and whose objective is to promote the development of its members while expanding and consolidating ties to the Pacific. In this article, I analyze the meaning of "The Pacific" from a historical perspective, and of the Pacific Alliance, focusing on Chile's foreign policy. I argue that "The Pacific" is a strategic construct created to strengthen the opening, diversification, and economic liberalization of the country. In the commercial sphere, it becomes a soft-balancing mechanism in response to protectionist ideas. In this argumentative line, and in the face of an uncertain commercial scenario, the Pacific Alliance is also a useful platform to defend the idea that free trade strengthens collective economic security.
