TY  - JOUR
AU  - Shepard, Bonnie L.
AU  - Becerra, Lidia Casas
TI  - Abortion policies and practices in Chile: Ambiguities and dilemmas
T2  - REPRODUCTIVE HEALTH MATTERS
M3  - Article
AB  - Abortion is not legal in Chile even to save the woman's life or health. This situation creates serious dilemmas and vulnerabilities for both women and medical practitioners. Abortion incidence has probably decreased since 1990, when data were last studied, due to increased use of contraception and lower fertility, and deaths and complication rates have fallen as well. Misoprostol is available, but Chilean hospitals are still using D&C for incomplete abortions. Although Chilean medical professionals should by low report illegal abortions to the authorities, less than 1% of women in hospital with abortion complications are reported. There ore two loopholes, one legal, one clinical. "Interruption of pregnancy" after 22 weeks of pregnancy is legal for medical reasons; this may save some women's lives but con also force prolongation of health-threatening pregnancies. Catholic clinical guidelines define interventions solely aimed at saving the woman's life, even if the fetus dies, not as abortion but "indirect abortion" and permissible. Since 1989, three bills to liberalise the low on therapeutic grounds have been unsuccessful. The political climate is not favourable to changing the low. Conservatives have also not succeeded in making the low more punitive, while the governing centre-left coalition is divided and the associated political risks ore considerable. (c) 2007 Reproductive Health Matters. All rights reserved.
