T2  - CUADERNOS DE VIVIENDA Y URBANISMO
M3  - Article
AB  - Difficulties in accessing adequate housing affect more than 1.1 billion people worldwide, representing nearly one quarter of the urban population. In Chile, this phenomenon is reflected in the growth of informal settlements, locally known as campamentos, where more than 120 000 families currently reside. This article examines the extent to which state-led strategies of in situ upgrading and relocation contribute to socio-spatial justice or, conversely, reproduce forms of territorial exclusion from the perspective of residents. Based on a comparative qualitative design of two case studies in Gran Valparaiso (2011-2024), the findings show that in situ upgrading tends to preserve territorial recognition, community networks, and urban accessibility. In contrast, relocation is associated with processes of displacement, stigmatization, and regressive effects linked to increased transport costs and reduced access to urban opportunities.
