TY  - JOUR
AU  - Ashley, Jennifer
AU  - Pribble, Jennifer
TI  - From Dignity to "Screw Them": Emotion, Policy, and Generic Appeals in Chile's Constitutional Television Campaigns
T2  - LATIN AMERICAN RESEARCH REVIEW
M3  - Article
AB  - Elections in many contemporary Latin American democracies unfold in a setting that complicates traditional political communication strategies. Indeed, many countries in the region are characterized by weak political parties, high levels of institutional distrust, and growing disdain for political elites. While a large body of literature has sought to explain which factors weaken parties and increase institutional distrust, less attention has been paid to the question of how these characteristics shape political communication. Drawing on the content of television advertisements created for Chile's constitutional plebiscite campaigns, and original interviews with the creative and political teams that designed the ads, we explore how each side communicated with voters; the issues they focused on; and to what extent they relied on partisan, policy, generic, or emotional appeals. The analysis identifies important changes in messaging across the three electoral contests and probes an explanation for this variation. We find that in the absence of partisan messages, the constitutional campaigns relied first on policy-based appeals but then transitioned to generic appeals, ultimately opting for "antipolitics" messaging. These changes resulted from the expansion of the electorate and growing distrust in the constitutional convention. The analysis also underscores that pro-status quo plebiscite campaigns are more likely to deploy negative emotional language than campaigns centered on change.Las elecciones en Am & eacute;rica Latina se desarrollan en un contexto que complica las estrategias tradicionales de comunicaci & oacute;n pol & iacute;tica. Esto se debe a que varios pa & iacute;ses de la regi & oacute;n cuentan con partidos pol & iacute;ticos d & eacute;biles, altos niveles de desconfianza institucional y un creciente desprecio hacia las & eacute;lites pol & iacute;ticas. Si bien existe una amplia literatura que busca explicar los factores que debilitan a los partidos y aumentan la desconfianza institucional, sabemos menos sobre c & oacute;mo estas caracter & iacute;sticas impactan la comunicaci & oacute;n pol & iacute;tica. Este art & iacute;culo analiza las franjas electorales de los tres plebiscitos constitucionales en Chile, junto con informaci & oacute;n de entrevistas originales realizadas a los equipos creativos y pol & iacute;ticos que dise & ntilde;aron las franjas. El an & aacute;lisis muestra c & oacute;mo cada equipo se comunic & oacute; con los votantes; qu & eacute; temas abordaron; y en qu & eacute; medida recurrieron a mensajes partidistas, apelaciones basadas en pol & iacute;ticas p & uacute;blicas, mensajes gen & eacute;ricos, o apelaciones emocionales. El an & aacute;lisis identifica cambios importantes en los mensajes a lo largo de las tres campa & ntilde;as electorales y propone una explicaci & oacute;n para esta variaci & oacute;n. Encontramos que, en ausencia de mensajes partidistas, las franjas recurrieron inicialmente a apelaciones basadas en pol & iacute;ticas p & uacute;blicas, pero luego hicieron una transici & oacute;n hacia mensajes gen & eacute;ricos, optando finalmente por un discurso "anti-pol & iacute;tica." Estos cambios fueron el resultado de la expansi & oacute;n del electorado y del creciente descontento con la convenci & oacute;n constitucional. El an & aacute;lisis tambi & eacute;n destaca que las franjas electorales de plebiscitos que defienden el statu quo tienen una mayor probabilidad de utilizar un lenguaje emocional, a diferencia de aquellas que promueven un mensaje de cambio.
