TY  - JOUR
AU  - Alvarez, Jessica Alejandra Alfaro
TI  - Women in small-scale fisheries and ancillary activities: Gendered labor, governance gaps, and policy implementation in Chile
T2  - MARINE POLICY
M3  - Article
AB  - Pre- and post-harvest work in small-scale fisheries has gained greater institutional recognition in Chile, especially since Law 21.370, but gaps persist between formal visibility, working conditions, and substantive equality. This article examines women's participation in these occupations in 17 coves in the Valparaiso Region, through a sequential mixed-methods design that combined surveys of 232 women and 10 semi-structured interviews. The findings show labor insertion marked by structural precariousness: 60.3% of respondents were between 42 and 64 years old, 37.1% had completed primary education, 74.8% identified as female household heads, and 98.8% of those engaged in ancillary activities had no employment contract. In addition, 27.2% used their home as a productive space, 47.0% reported chronic illnesses, 56.0% had witnessed acts of intimidation or discrimination, and 86.2% perceived "machismo" in the workplace. The analysis distinguishes between kinship ties, understood as empirical relationships that facilitate entry into the sector and intergenerational continuity, and familism, understood as a moral economy that may naturalize women's availability, precarious work, and care burdens. This distinction helps explain how women's inclusion can coexist with partial recognition and persistent subordination. The article shows that formal recognition requires gender-sensitive infrastructure, social protection, violence prevention, and implementation mechanisms capable of translating Law 21.370 into material redistribution and effective representation.
